# Periodic cache.db re-download for the stdio/serve deployment — every 15
# minutes. Replaces the Pub/Sub-pushed /internal/reload custom_route used by
# the streamable-http deployment (entrypoint.sh), which has no equivalent on
# a stdio-only server: there is no HTTP route for a push to land on, so this
# service polls GCS on a timer instead. gcs_sync.py --init-cache is the same
# call entrypoint-stdio.sh makes at startup; a fetch failure here is
# non-fatal (see its own logging) and simply leaves the previous cache.db in
# place until the next tick.
#
# Chained with verify_cache.py: a successful re-download atomically renames
# cache.db onto a new inode (gcs_sync.py's tmp-file + rename), which
# invalidates CacheStore's verified-generation sidecar. Running
# verify_cache.py right after re-warms that sidecar so the next per-request
# jquants-mcp child (mcp-stdio serve spawns a fresh one per claude.ai
# message) can report cache_integrity without repeating the multi-second
# PRAGMA quick_check itself. supercronic runs each job line via `sh -c`, so
# `&&` works, and it skips a tick whose previous run is still executing, so
# this cannot pile up even if a download runs long.
#
# REQUIRES the Cloud Run service to run with CPU always allocated
# (`--no-cpu-throttling`), not the default request-based throttling. Under
# request-based billing, Cloud Run suspends CPU on a warm instance between
# requests — supercronic cannot execute this job on schedule if no request
# happens to be in flight at the tick, and even if it starts, a multi-GB
# download that outlives the next request's end gets throttled mid-transfer.
# That is the exact failure mode entrypoint-stdio.sh's synchronous
# startup download exists to avoid; this cron job needs the same CPU
# guarantee to be reliable on its own schedule. See jquants-mcp#570 review.
*/15 * * * * python /app/scripts/gcs_sync.py --init-cache && python /app/scripts/verify_cache.py
